      "message": "Here\u0027s a fix generated by Gemini. Review the code for correctness and make sure it works with a device:\n\n----\n\nThe cleanest architectural fix is **Constructor Injection**. By passing the specific IN, OUT, and Interrupt endpoints into the `UsbComm` constructor, the class becomes truly reusable, and `BootromComm` can stop bypassing the high-level `Send()` and `Receive()` methods.\n\nHere are the specific changes to make:\n\n### 1\\. Update `usb_comm.h`\n\nRemove the hardcoded constants and add the endpoints as member variables initialized via the constructor.\n\n```c\n// Remove these globally hardcoded endpoints:\n// constexpr uint8_t kEpOutBulk \u003d 0x01;\n// constexpr uint8_t kEpInBulk \u003d 0x81;\n// constexpr uint8_t kEpInInt \u003d 0x82;\n\nclass UsbComm {\n public:\n  // Update the constructor to accept endpoints:\n  UsbComm(libusb_context *ctx, uint16_t vid, uint16_t pid, \n          uint8_t ep_out, uint8_t ep_in, uint8_t ep_int \u003d 0);\n  \n  // ... (keep existing methods)\n\n private:\n  libusb_context *ctx_ \u003d nullptr;\n  std::unique_ptr\u003clibusb_device_handle, decltype(\u0026libusb_close)\u003e handle_{\n      nullptr, \u0026libusb_close};\n  uint16_t vid_;\n  uint16_t pid_;\n  bool kernel_driver_detached_ \u003d false;\n\n  // Add the endpoint members:\n  uint8_t ep_out_;\n  uint8_t ep_in_;\n  uint8_t ep_int_;\n};\n\n```\n\n### 2\\. Update `usb_comm.cc`\n\nWire up the new constructor and replace the hardcoded constants in `Send`, `Receive`, and `Wait`.\n\n```c\n// 1. Update the constructor:\nUsbComm::UsbComm(libusb_context *ctx, uint16_t vid, uint16_t pid, \n                 uint8_t ep_out, uint8_t ep_in, uint8_t ep_int)\n    : ctx_(ctx), vid_(vid), pid_(pid), \n      ep_out_(ep_out), ep_in_(ep_in), ep_int_(ep_int) {}\n\n// 2. In Send(), replace kEpOutBulk with ep_out_:\nint rc \u003d libusb_bulk_transfer(handle_.get(), ep_out_,\n                              const_cast\u003cuint8_t *\u003e(data.data()), data.size(),\n                              \u0026transferred, kTransferTimeout.count());\n\n// 3. In Receive(), replace kEpInBulk with ep_in_:\nint rc \u003d libusb_bulk_transfer(handle_.get(), ep_in_, data.data(), data.size(),\n                              \u0026transferred, kTransferTimeout.count());\n\n// 4. In Wait(), replace kEpInInt with ep_int_:\nint rc \u003d libusb_interrupt_transfer(handle_.get(), ep_int_, irq_buf.data(),\n                                   irq_buf.size(), \u0026transferred,\n                                   kTransferTimeout.count());\n\n```\n\n### 3\\. Clean up `bootrom.cc`\n\nNow that `UsbComm` is properly encapsulated, `BootromComm` no longer needs to use the lower-level `BulkWrite` and `BulkRead` methods. You can update `DoUsbTransfer` to use the cleaner abstractions:\n\n```c\nstd::expected\u003cstd::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e, BootromError\u003e BootromComm::DoUsbTransfer(\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e tx_cmd, size_t min_expected_rx_size,\n    std::chrono::milliseconds timeout) {\n  DumpRawCmd(tx_cmd);\n\n  // Replace direct BulkWrite with Send:\n  if (auto res \u003d usb_.Send(tx_cmd); !res)\n    return std::unexpected(BootromError::kUsbWriteFailed);\n\n  std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e ret(kMaxPayloadSize + min_expected_rx_size, 0);\n  \n  // Replace direct BulkRead with Receive:\n  auto read_res \u003d usb_.Receive(ret);\n  if (!read_res || read_res.value() \u003c\u003d 0)\n    return std::unexpected(BootromError::kUsbReadFailed);\n\n  // ... (keep rest of function identical)\n}\n\n```\n\n### 4\\. Wire it together in `et171_flash.cc`\n\nFinally, update the instantiations of `UsbComm` to pass the correct endpoints based on whether you are talking to the EC App or the Bootrom.\n\n```c\n// In ConnectToBootloader:\n// EC App uses 0x01 (OUT), 0x81 (IN), and 0x82 (INT)\nUsbComm app_comm(usb_ctx, kVidGoogle, kPidEgisApp, 0x01, 0x81, 0x82);\n\n// In main():\n// Bootloader uses 0x02 (OUT) and 0x81 (IN). Pass 0 for INT since it\u0027s unused.\negis::UsbComm boot_comm(usb_ctx.get(), egis::kVidEgis,\n                        egis::kPidEgisBootloader, 0x02, 0x81, 0);\n\n```",
